[Music]  this morning on the dish the foods of  eastern europe may conjure up images of  potato and borscht and the cuisine isn't  as globally renowned as that of italy  and france but ukrainian chef and writer  olea hercules is working to change that  perception by tirelessly documenting the  recipes and food of her homeland and its  neighboring regions  mts taya has her story  olia hercules knows how to feed a crowd  at her pop-up in london she delights  diners with an array of ukrainian dishes  i'm super super excited to have you here  enjoy  but it's the humble and hearty dumpling  which captivates taste buds here and  more dumplings are coming  at her home kitchen in the british  capital she shows us the art of making  the perfect parcel so you grab this  corner first okay and then we just just  stick it here  so just like just yeah just like that  and then thank you that's not the most  beautiful dumpling ever made  hercules says her journey to chef and  cookbook author truly began when her  family moved from ukraine to cyprus when  she was just 12 years old later moving  to london she gave up a career in  journalism to work in some of the city's  best kitchens but it was the food of her  childhood which she wanted to share with  the world a lot of people do maybe see  eastern european food as quite  monolithic it's borscht it's dumpling  it's potatoes it's potatoes and cabbage  but clearly not i mean  you were able to write three cookbooks  about it right now exactly  2014 was supposed to be one of the  happiest years in hercules life she had  just signed her first book deal  but her joy soon turned to horror as  russian tanks rolled into eastern  ukraine not far from her parents home it  was the most terrifying time of my life  you know uh not a fearful person at all  but uh when that happened because  especially because my family just an  hour and a half away from crimea so  if it was to move forward you know would  be next so of course you know kind of  waking up it was it was terrifying it  was the worst time  for all of us i think  um  but  it also triggered something inside you  to want to preserve  something so personal to you which is  your family's recipes  yeah absolutely i don't know  stars aligned in a way that  the whole  first cookbook  deal  mamushka became a bestseller and won  major awards it was followed up with  caucuses a culinary journey through  georgia azerbaijan and beyond and now  hercules has a third cookbook which was  published this year called summer  kitchens  what they are um  one  room little houses which look very  similar to your main house  um and they're usually situated just a  few steps away you know in your yard and  this is where you'd cook during the  summer because it's cooler to do it  there is when all of the jars would come  out and all of your gluts like all of  the produce fruit and vegetables would  be fermented pickled etc so it's you  know a huge operation i was gonna say  this is a lot of work there you go  you've got this very special kind of  kitchen workshop and i wanted to look at  how ukraine is  very diverse but also united by this  tradition  so even though food up north and down  south may vary we have this summer  kitchen where all of the cooking is  being done  dumplings are at the heart of hercules  cooking like manti filled with lamb and  fenugreek  gulhelun from central asia bursting with  sweet potato and apricot on yogurt  then  there's her favorite  these are called varenike and what's  inside the berenike  so we've got potato  and  cheese  dills yeah  and i actually added a little bit of  spinach because i had to use it up so  you know it's traditional just with a  little bit  that is so good  i was not expecting such strong flavors  to come out of that what's it like when  your family's together and you're  preparing a meal and then sharing a meal  with each other  so  vast amounts of food and the huge  extended family would get together and  you know sometimes they'd be laughing  and sometimes they'd be crying and  sometimes some singing would happen and  you know  and i feel like i'm just forever chasing  that feeling and maybe that's why i  became a chef and a writer you know just  trying to  bring back all of the  emotions that that kind of situation  gave me when i was a kid  for cbs this morning saturday  london  she gave us such an insight into not  only the culture but you know a sense of  like longing for her homeland and she  just made me very hungry  [Laughter]  i heated up leftovers which are not  nearly as good as what she made  incredible yeah it's very very  good for you leftovers too we might have  fresh vegetables he likes in the morning

